Connect to PostgreSQL
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line knowledge
Understanding of what a database is
Access to a Linux machine with sudo privileges
Internet connection for package installation
Learning Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Install PostgreSQL client tools on Linux
Configure database connection credentials
Connect to PostgreSQL using the psql command-line tool
Verify successful database connections
List available databases in PostgreSQL
Environment Setup
Note: Al Nafi provides a bare-metal Linux cloud machine. Click the "Start Lab" button to access your environment. You will install all necessary tools during this lab.

System Information
Operating System: Linux (Ubuntu/Debian-based)
Required Tools: PostgreSQL client and server
Estimated Time: 45 minutes
Task 1: Install PostgreSQL
Step 1: Update Package Repository
Before installing any software, update your system's package list:

sudo apt update
What this does: Refreshes the list of available packages and their versions.

Step 2: Install PostgreSQL
Install both the PostgreSQL server and client tools:

sudo apt install postgresql postgresql-contrib -y
Components installed:

postgresql: The database server
postgresql-contrib: Additional utilities and extensions
-y: Automatically answers "yes" to installation prompts
Step 3: Verify Installation
Check that PostgreSQL is installed and running:

sudo systemctl status postgresql
Expected output: You should see "active (running)" in green text.

If the service is not running, start it:

sudo systemctl start postgresql
Step 4: Check PostgreSQL Version
Verify the installed version:

psql --version
Expected output: Something like psql (PostgreSQL) 14.x or similar.

Task 2: Configure Database Credentials and Connect
Step 1: Switch to PostgreSQL User
PostgreSQL creates a default system user called postgres during installation. Switch to this user:

sudo -i -u postgres
What this does: Logs you in as the postgres user, which has default access to the database.

Step 2: Access PostgreSQL Prompt
Connect to PostgreSQL using the psql client:

psql
Expected output: You should see a prompt like postgres=#

This means you're now inside the PostgreSQL interactive terminal.

Step 3: List All Databases
Once connected, list all available databases:

\l
Expected output: A table showing databases including:

postgres (default database)
template0 and template1 (template databases)
Step 4: Display Connection Information
Check your current connection details:

\conninfo
Expected output: Shows your current database, user, socket, and port information.

Step 5: Create a Test Database
Create a new database to verify you have proper permissions:

CREATE DATABASE testdb;
Expected output: CREATE DATABASE

List databases again to confirm:

\l
You should now see testdb in the list.

Step 6: Connect to Your New Database
Switch to the newly created database:

\c testdb
Expected output: You are now connected to database "testdb" as user "postgres".

Step 7: Exit PostgreSQL
Exit the psql prompt:

\q
Return to your regular user account:

exit
Task 3: Create a Custom Database User
Step 1: Create a New PostgreSQL User
Switch back to the postgres user and create a custom database user:

sudo -u postgres createuser --interactive
Prompts you'll see:

Enter name of role to add: labuser
Shall the new role be a superuser? n
Step 2: Set Password for New User
Access PostgreSQL as the postgres user:

sudo -u postgres psql
Set a password for your new user:

ALTER USER labuser WITH PASSWORD 'securepassword123';
Note: In production, use strong passwords. This is just for learning.

Step 3: Grant Database Access
Grant the new user access to the test database:

GRANT ALL PRIVILEGES ON DATABASE testdb TO labuser;
Exit psql:

\q
Step 4: Configure Password Authentication
Edit the PostgreSQL authentication configuration:

sudo nano /etc/postgresql/*/main/pg_hba.conf
Find this line:

local   all             all                                     peer
Change it to:

local   all             all                                     md5
What this does: Enables password authentication for local connections.

Save and exit (Ctrl+X, then Y, then Enter).

Step 5: Restart PostgreSQL
Apply the configuration changes:

sudo systemctl restart postgresql
Step 6: Test Connection with New User
Connect using your new user credentials:

psql -U labuser -d testdb -h localhost
Prompt: Enter password: securepassword123

Expected output: testdb=> prompt

List databases as the new user:

\l
Exit:

\q
Verification
Checklist
Verify you've completed all tasks:

PostgreSQL is installed:

psql --version
PostgreSQL service is running:

sudo systemctl status postgresql
Can connect as postgres user:

sudo -u postgres psql -c "\conninfo"
Test database exists:

sudo -u postgres psql -c "\l" | grep testdb
Custom user can connect:

psql -U labuser -d testdb -h localhost -c "\conninfo"
(Enter password when prompted)

Expected Results
All commands should execute without errors. You should see:

Version information displayed
Active/running status for PostgreSQL
Connection information showing successful connections
Database listings including your testdb
Troubleshooting Tips
Issue: "psql: command not found"
Solution: PostgreSQL client not installed. Run:

sudo apt install postgresql-client
Issue: "connection refused"
Solution: PostgreSQL service not running. Start it:

sudo systemctl start postgresql
Issue: "authentication failed"
Solution:

Verify password is correct
Check pg_hba.conf has md5 authentication enabled
Restart PostgreSQL after configuration changes
Issue: "permission denied"
Solution: Make sure you're using sudo for system commands or switched to the postgres user.

Conclusion
Congratulations! You have successfully:

Installed PostgreSQL database server and client tools
Configured and verified the PostgreSQL service
Connected to PostgreSQL using the default postgres user
Created a custom database and database user
Configured password-based authentication
Verified connections using different authentication methods
Why This Matters: PostgreSQL is one of the most popular open-source relational databases used in production environments worldwide. The skills you learned today—installing, configuring, and connecting to PostgreSQL—are fundamental for any database administrator, backend developer, or data engineer. You now have a working PostgreSQL installation that you can use for future labs and projects.

